{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner ruling on the bootstrap route for fc2548ae, answering checker 51b05984. Route (b), in the same head, before the exact-head review. This replaces point 1 of aa90926d.\n\n1. Migration reads the current parent, not the history. When a journal has a confirmed receipt and no native state, one migration pass reads the confirmed commit at depth 1 with the blob:none filter, so it gets the commit and its current trees only. It verifies them against the stored receipt (exact commit, scope, incarnation, head path and tree hashes), extracts the frontier and stores native state. That pass archives no entries, so the whole pass budget is free for the read. The next pass is an ordinary native pass.\n\n2. Its bound. The migration read has its own fixed byte bound and is charged on the actual bytes in the archive budget. No cap in ARCHIVE_BOUNDS changes, and the stopgap read keeps its 8,487-byte allowance. The bound admits at least 16 KiB of trees under the unchanged budgets. The current trees I measured are 616 to 8,698 bytes (462f064f), and they stop growing while a journal is stalled. The head states the admitted bound as a measured value. If 16 KiB does not fit under the unchanged budgets, report that before the review request.\n\n3. The complete-closure bootstrap goes. With point 1 it has no use. Do not keep the stopgap read as a fallback. A journal over the migration bound refuses with its state intact, as before.\n\n4. Acceptance, in addition to 3 of 51b05984: a fixture with a legacy journal of 37 passes and about 8,700 tree bytes migrates in one pass and then archives in native passes; a journal of 6 passes does the same; a journal over the bound refuses and keeps its state; a wrong receipt, scope or head refuses.\n\nNo closure measurement is needed for this route. The planner deploys after exact-head review, then reads the journals again.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}